              "message": "Review: a revised artefact starts a new round\n\nA task under review can go back to in_progress, through task.update or a\nreject with request_revision. When task.complete then submitted a new\nartefact on a task that requires review, the open review kept its\ndecisions, so under all_approve or quorum:<n> an approval of the first\nartefact counted towards the rule on the second, and the task could\ncomplete with an artefact one of its approvers never saw.\n\nThe completion now opens a new round of the same review: the rule and\nthe reviewers stay, and the decisions start empty. The completer, the\nnew artefact's author, is left out of the round's named reviewers, as it\nis when the first review opens; a completion that would leave none is\nrefused with -32011. The earlier round's decisions stay on the chain.\nBoth coordinators, with the same tests in each.\n\nprofiles/review.md and SPECIFICATION.md state the rule, and what it\nleaves as it was. A copy of a decision sent again is evaluated like any\naccepted call, and one that carries approved_artefact_digest is refused\nagainst any other artefact, which a new test shows. A broadcast reviewer\nlets any member decide, and review.request can still choose a review's\nreviewers, so a deployment that must keep an agent from choosing its own\nwithholds review.request from it, as the mcp-gate template does.\n\nchap-analytics replays the chain the same way: a revised artefact on a\ntask that requires review is a new pass, without its completer among\nthe reviewers. Its test that asserted the old pooling now asserts the new\npass, and so do the tests of the production, support-desk and\noutbound-approval templates that read a revised review: its decisions\nstart empty, and the task's decision log keeps the rejection.",
