              "message": "Patch: cap the size and depth of what an override can produce\n\nThe JSON Patch engine capped operations (1,000) and the patched\ndocument's values (100,000), and nothing capped its size or depth. Each\ncopy of the root doubles the document, long strings included, so\nfifteen copies grew a 64 KiB artefact to about 2 GiB within the value\ncap. A patch that puts one value inside another nests the document\nwithout limit: a few hundred levels made the Python coordinator answer\nwith an internal error, and a few thousand left the TypeScript\ncoordinator unable to save the workspace or send its reply. Any\nreviewer could send either as a decide.override.\n\nThe engine now also counts the UTF-8 bytes of the document's strings\nand keys, and refuses a patch that grows them past the limit with\n-32012. The coordinators set the limit to their max_envelope_bytes,\n1 MiB by default: an artefact that arrives in a request holds no more\ntext than the request's size. TypeScript counts with Buffer where Node\nhas it, and by hand elsewhere; Python counts with surrogatepass; the\ntwo give the same numbers, so they refuse the same patches. A document\nnested deeper than 64 levels, counted as a request's nesting is, is\nrefused the same way.\n\nEach operation keeps the counts in step by counting what it puts in\nand what it takes out, so the values and bytes are exact after every\noperation, and a patch costs what it changes, whatever the document's\nsize. A move takes its value out and puts it back without copying it.\nThe depth is a bound raised by what each operation puts in, and it\npasses the limit only when the document does. A thousand edits to a\n1 MB artefact took about 800 ms when the document was measured after\neach one, and take a few now.\n\nA copy, and a move to a deeper place or onto the whole document, costs\na pass over the value it carries. Between them, a patch's copies and\nsuch moves may carry three times the value and size limits, which\nbounds the work one override can ask for: copying a list of 100,000\nempty objects onto itself a thousand times took about 7 seconds of CPU\nin TypeScript and 40 in Python, and is now refused at the fourth copy.\n\nThe engine puts in copies of the values a patch gives it. Before, when\none operation changed a value an earlier one had added, the patch kept\nin the override record changed with it.\n\nchap-analytics' copy of the engine, which rebuilds corrected artefacts,\nhas the same limits with 64 MiB of text, so it rebuilds every override\na coordinator accepted while that coordinator's max_envelope_bytes is\nat most 64 MiB, the code gate's 16 MiB included.\n\nTests, in each of the three engines: the size at the boundary for a\nreplaced value, a copy, an added key, a moved key and a key in UTF-8\nbytes; the value count at its limit; the order of the refusals; copies\nwhose source moves along the array; the depth at the limit, values\nstacked past it, a move or a copy carried past it, and a document that\nstarts deeper; what copies and moves carry; a patch that stays as it\nwas sent; and a seeded comparison with measuring the whole document\nafter every operation. TypeScript also counts UTF-8 bytes where there\nis no Buffer. Shared vectors and refusal messages for both\ncoordinators, and chap-analytics runs the vectors too. Each coordinator\nrefuses the deep override and keeps the task as it was.",
