              "message": "MCP gate: the assistant's connection acts only as the assistant\n\nThe mcp-gate template served every CHAP tool on /mcp, and every call\nnamed its own `from`. With signatures off, the assistant could call\nchap.decide.approve in the human's name, whose URI workspace.describe\nshows, and approve its own work, though the README said it could not.\nThe tool filter did not help: both MCP servers left an excluded tool out\nof the list and still ran it when it was called by name, and the A2A\nexecutors took no filter at all.\n\n- makeChapMcpServer and make_chap_mcp_server refuse a tool their filter\n  excludes, and take a new `participant` option: every call is made as\n  that participant, and a call that names another `from` is refused.\n- The A2A executors, in both languages, take the Agent Card's skill\n  filter and refuse the skills it excludes, with -32601.\n- The templates' /mcp sets `participant` to the agent and offers only\n  AGENT_MCP_TOOLS: the tools the agent's own work needs, and the\n  decision tools, so the coordinator still refuses and records an\n  attempt on the agent's own work. Every other tool is refused. This\n  covers mcp-gate and code-gate, which turn /mcp on, and production with\n  CHAP_MCP set. A configuration that turns /mcp on without agent.uri is\n  refused at start.\n- The mcp-gate README says what this covers, that POST /chap still\n  accepts a call in any member's name unless signatures are required,\n  and that requiring them closes /mcp too, since its calls are unsigned.\n\nCallers that pass neither option see no change. The template test that\nexpected all 39 tools now checks the agent's set, and new tests cover\nthe human's name, the filters and the option in both languages.",
