          "context": "## What was asked\n\nBuild the six fixes in FIX_PLAN_0.3.4.md, from the code review of\n29 October, and release them as 0.3.4 with `chap-analytics` 0.2.5. Nothing\nelse in the packages or the generated apps may change.\n\n## What changed, and why\n\n- **Review rounds.** A task completed again after a revision kept the\n  decisions on its earlier artefact, so under `all_approve` or `quorum:<n>`\n  an approval of the first artefact counted for the second. The completion\n  now opens a new round: same reviewers and rule, no decisions, a new\n  `requested_at`. The completer is left out of the round's named reviewers,\n  and a completion that would leave none is refused with `-32011`. The\n  profile now says what the round leaves as it was: a copy of a decision\n  sent again is evaluated like any accepted call, and one that carries\n  `approved_artefact_digest` is refused against any other artefact; a\n  broadcast reviewer lets any member decide; `review.request` can still\n  choose a review's reviewers.\n- **Patch limits.** A `decide.override` patch could grow an artefact to\n  gigabytes with `copy`, or nest it without limit. The engine now refuses,\n  after any operation, more string and key text than `max_envelope_bytes`\n  or more than 64 levels, and a patch whose copies and deeper moves carry\n  more than three times the value and size limits. The counts are kept per\n  operation, so a patch costs what it changes. The engine also puts in\n  copies of the patch's values, so the patch kept in the override record\n  stays as it was sent. chap-analytics' copy of the engine has the same\n  limits, with 64 MiB of text.\n- **MCP gate.** The template's `/mcp` served every tool and let each call\n  name its own `from`, so the assistant could approve in the human's name.\n  Both MCP servers now refuse a tool their filter excludes and take a\n  `participant` that every call is made as; the templates' `/mcp` sets it\n  to the agent and offers only `AGENT_MCP_TOOLS`. The A2A executors refuse\n  skills their filter excludes. The README says what `POST /chap` still\n  allows while signatures are off.\n- **Supplied ids.** `whisper.ask`, `deliberate.open` and `handoff.propose`\n  refuse, with `-32602`, an id already in use, one that is not a string,\n  and the names `__proto__`, `constructor` and `prototype`. The TypeScript\n  coordinator lost an object stored under `__proto__` when it saved the\n  workspace.\n- **zod 3.** The MCP package uses `.passthrough()`, which zod 3 and 4 both\n  have.\n- **Python A2A card.** The binding is named `JSONRPC`, the value a2a-sdk\n  clients look for.\n- **Release.** Versions, the template pins, the lock file's own version\n  lines, both changelogs, CITATION.cff's date, and the profile and\n  SECURITY.md text each fix needs.\n\n## How it was tested\n\n- TypeScript: build, typecheck and every package's tests, with SQLite\n  required, on Node 20, 22 and 24, the Node 20 run from a fresh clone\n  installed with `npm ci`.\n- Python 3.10: chap-coordinator, the five bridges, chap-analytics and the\n  starter, also in a fresh environment with the latest dev dependencies.\n- The conformance harness against both reference servers, and the\n  differential fuzzer on 25 seeds: all agree.\n- The version, schema, MCP-schema, catalogue, error-code, em-dash and link\n  checks.\n- Each template generated with the packages linked, with its own tests and\n  `diff-profiles`.\n- A dry run of the publish: the four npm tarballs packed, installed into\n  projects generated from the packed `create-chap-app`, with their tests and\n  the MCP server's start; the seven Python projects built, passing\n  `twine check`, installed into a clean environment, with the runbook's\n  checks, the Python templates, the starter and the profile explorer.\n- The Starter workflow's jobs on Linux: its demos, the agent and desk path,\n  and the desk test in a real DOM.\n- The four reference servers start, and both A2A cards name `JSONRPC`.\n- Every new test fails on the code before its fix.\n- The patch engines: seeded comparisons with measuring the whole document\n  after every operation, in all three engines, and between TypeScript,\n  Python and the 0.3.3 engine; mutation runs, in which every deliberate\n  fault in the counting is caught by the tests; and timing of the costliest\n  patch shapes, each now well under a second.\n- Six independent reviews, three of them of the patch engines alone. Their\n  findings are fixed in these commits, except those under \"Left for later\".\n\n## Look closely at\n\n- `opTaskComplete` in both coordinators: the new round, and the completer\n  left out of it.\n- The patch engines: the counts each operation keeps, the depth bound, and\n  the carried limit.\n- `server.mjs` in the templates: `AGENT_MCP_TOOLS` and the `participant`\n  on `/mcp`.\n- The 0.3.4 changelog entry, which says these fixes ship as a patch release\n  because they are security fixes.\n\n## Left for later\n\nThese were found during the work and are unchanged from 0.3.3. None is made\nworse by 0.3.4.\n\n- **Replayed decisions.** A copy of a signed decision is evaluated again, as\n  any accepted call is, so it can count in a later round. One that carries\n  `approved_artefact_digest` is refused against another artefact. Refusing\n  copies of decisions outright is a design decision for the replay work on\n  the roadmap.\n- **Who chooses a review's reviewers.** A member who may call\n  `review.request` can still add reviewers to an open review, or open one\n  with any reviewers and rule on a task back in progress; a broadcast\n  reviewer lets any member decide, the completer included. Refusing\n  decisions from an artefact's author, broadcast included, needs the author\n  recorded on the review.\n- **A round nobody can settle.** A `quorum:<n>` round with fewer than `n`\n  named reviewers, or one whose reviewers have all left, is accepted. The\n  first round has the same gap.\n- **Keys JavaScript treats specially.** An idempotency key of `__proto__`\n  meets the same object-key problem as the ids above, in the store and in\n  memory.\n- **Generated ids** are not checked against existing ones; this matters\n  only with `deterministicIds`, in tests and scenarios.\n- **Patch refusal parity.** Python quotes a non-ASCII path segment as `\\u`\n  escapes, and TypeScript as the character. A `diff` that is an object\n  completes the override in Python and is refused in TypeScript. A `path`\n  of `null` replaces the whole artefact in TypeScript and is an internal\n  error in Python.\n- **The teaching server** in `reference/core-plus-review` has its own patch\n  engine without these limits, and does not open new review rounds.\n- **chap-analytics** treats only a patch refusal as a patch that does not\n  apply; a document an in-process caller made deeper than Python can follow\n  still raises.\n- **Docs.** The code-gate README and AGENT_INSTRUCTIONS.md say the agent can\n  read its tasks and the chain over `/mcp`; with signatures required, which\n  is the code gate's default, every unsigned MCP call is refused. The MCP\n  and A2A package READMEs do not document `participant` or the skill\n  filter.\n- **Version rules.** ROADMAP.md says a change to what the specification\n  requires is a minor release, and SECURITY.md says security fixes ship as\n  patch releases. A line in ROADMAP.md would settle which wins."
